# 21. THE COMPLETE USER EXPERIENCE

Design all key journeys before implementation.

### Journey A: Parent discovering a toy
Open the app, search naturally, apply age and budget filters, compare recommendations, inspect safety information, add a toy to a wishlist and proceed to a secure adult-managed checkout.

### Journey B: Child exploring
Enter a parent-approved child interface, discover suitable toy categories, explore curated activities, receive safe recommendations and earn non-monetary recognition for optional challenges.

### Journey C: Birthday gifting
Create a birthday wishlist, invite family members through adult-managed sharing, reduce duplicate gifts and order from eligible sellers.

### Journey D: Selling a pre-loved toy
An adult creates a listing, uploads real photographs, describes condition and missing components, reviews transaction rules, publishes the listing and tracks the order.

### Journey E: Toy collector
Create a collection, catalog owned items, track purchase history, identify possible matching listings and review evidence-based market estimates.

### Journey F: Toy safety
Search a product by name, model or identifier, retrieve available official safety notices, inspect the source and date, and report a suspected issue.

### Journey G: Educator planning activities
Select an age range, topic, duration and available materials to find appropriate activities, printable instructions and supervision requirements.

Every journey must have loading states, useful empty states, input validation, understandable error messages and a successful completion state.

